{#
  The bank payment confirmation's BODY — the A4 sheet, extracted so that ONE markup serves two
  carriers: `ua_bank_payment_confirmation.html` (the document as a file of its own) and
  `ua_bank_receipt_in_app.html` (the same document inside a phone screen). The extraction is
  the alternative its own header comment always named — two copies of this markup would be two
  documents that drift, which is the failure the in-app archetype exists to rule out.

  A FRAGMENT, NOT AN ARCHETYPE: `templates/*.jinja` renders on its own by nothing. Every
  `data-field` of the class lives here, so both carriers collect the same field set from one
  source; the in-app carrier adds only its own chrome fields on top.
#}
{# ⛔ NO `data-document` HERE, and the absence is the design: this body serves two carriers,
   and the ROOT belongs to whichever template is the whole render — the A4 wrapper marks a
   fit-content div around this include, the phone screen marks itself. A root in the body and
   another in a wrapper would be two roots in one render, which the one-root sweep forbids. #}
<div class="page">

  {# 👁 The header carries the bank's own requisites. The issuer is drawn per document from
     `banks` in config/vendors.json — a single issuer on every image would teach a consumer the
     name instead of the field, which is the lesson the acquirer field on the receipt already
     paid for. #}
  <header class="head">
    <div class="bank">
      <div class="bank-name" data-field="bank_name">{{ bank_name }}</div>
      <div class="muted">{{ bank_code_label }} <span data-field="bank_code">{{ bank_code }}</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stamp-slot"></div>
  </header>

  {# 🔴 THE TITLE IS NOT A TYPE MARKER. 👁 One bank names the same kind of document two ways, and
     on one observed image the application's screen heading and the document heading inside it
     disagree while carrying the same number. A classifier keying on this string is keying on a
     coin toss, which is why both wordings are drawn from. #}
  <div class="title-row">
    <h1 class="title" data-field="title">{{ title }}</h1>
    <div class="doc-code">{{ document_code_label }} <span data-field="document_code">{{ document_code }}</span></div>
  </div>

  {# THE DATE, under one of the three captions the labelling contract accepts as the payment
     date. config/policy.yaml names the CONCEPT — the date the funds left the payer's account —
     and `document_types.payment_confirmation.payment_date` in config/labelling-schema.yaml maps
     captions onto it. The three captions that must never be read as the payment date are
     therefore never printed here: a document carrying one would be a document whose payment
     date the contract says cannot be read, which no honest archetype should produce. #}
  <div class="row meta">
    <span class="caption">{{ date_caption }}</span>
    {# Date and time as two spans with no label between them, the way the receipt prints them.
       👁 One observed caption is «Дата і час операції» — one caption over both values — so a
       separate «Час» label would be a caption no document was seen to carry. #}
    <span>
      <span data-field="date">{{ date }}</span>
      <span data-field="time">{{ time }}</span>
    </span>
  </div>

  {# 👁 THE LARGEST BLOCK OF THE DOCUMENT — 6/8 lay the two parties out in two columns. ⚠️ The
     length of a code tells you the KIND of code and not the kind of party: 10 digits is a
     РНОКПП, which a private individual and a sole trader both have, and 8 is a ЄДРПОУ. A rule
     reading "ten digits, so not a business" is false — 👁 a sole-trader payee with a ten-digit
     code was observed. #}
  <section class="parties">
    <div class="party">
      <div class="caption">{{ party_labels.payer_label }}</div>
      <div class="party-name" data-field="payer_name">{{ payer.name }}</div>
      {% if payer.code %}
      <div class="muted">{{ party_labels.code_label }} <span data-field="payer_code">{{ payer.code }}</span></div>
      {% endif %}
      {% if payer.account %}
      <div class="muted">{{ party_labels.account_label }} <span data-field="payer_account">{{ payer.account }}</span></div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    <div class="party">
      <div class="caption">{{ party_labels.payee_label }}</div>
      <div class="party-name" data-field="payee_name">{{ payee.name }}</div>
      {% if payee.code %}
      <div class="muted">{{ party_labels.code_label }} <span data-field="payee_code">{{ payee.code }}</span></div>
      {% endif %}
      {% if payee.account %}
      <div class="muted">{{ party_labels.account_label }} <span data-field="payee_account">{{ payee.account }}</span></div>
      {% endif %}
      {# THE SECOND FORM OF EMPTINESS: the caption is printed and there is nothing under it.
         👁 Observed on the recipient's bank, alongside two other captioned-but-empty fields on
         one document. The caption stays because that is what makes the field empty rather than
         absent — and the value element is omitted, so no box points at nothing. #}
      <div class="muted">{{ party_labels.bank_label }}
        {% if payee.bank %}<span data-field="payee_bank">{{ payee.bank }}</span>{% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# 🔴 THE MONEY, AND THE DIVERGENCE THIS ARCHETYPE EXISTS TO MAKE MEASURABLE.
     👁 0 of 8 observed confirmations carry a single amount: every one prints the operation
     amount and the fee as separate fields, and 👁 1 of 8 also prints their sum — in the largest
     type on the page.

     📄 The amount of the OPERATION is the transfer, which the National Bank's instruction on
     non-cash settlements makes a requisite and which does not include the fee. So the most
     salient number on the page is NOT the amount of the claim. An extractor reaching for the
     biggest figure is wrong by exactly the fee, and it is now visible whether it resists.

     The fee is NON-ZERO on about a third of documents, matching the observation. It is a bank's
     charge for a service rather than anything a benefit category covers, so it is labelled and
     never reimbursed. #}
  <section class="money">
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ amount_caption }}</span>
      <span class="amount" data-field="amount">{{ amount }}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ fee_caption }}</span>
      <span data-field="fee">{{ fee }}</span>
    </div>
    {% if total_charged %}
    <div class="row grand">
      <span class="caption">{{ total_caption }}</span>
      <span data-field="total_charged">{{ total_charged }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if amount_in_words %}
    <div class="words">
      <span class="caption">{{ amount_in_words_caption }}: </span>
      <span data-field="amount_in_words">{{ amount_in_words }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {# 🔴 THE PAYMENT PURPOSE, WHICH NEVER SAYS WHAT WAS BOUGHT — 👁 0 of 7 documents carrying the
     field. It names ANOTHER DOCUMENT: an invoice by number and date. That is the observation
     behind `proves_subject: false` for this type in config/policy.yaml, and it is what makes
     the dominant pair a pair — an invoice proves the subject, this proves the payment.

     Absent entirely on an internet-acquiring document (👁 1/8 has no such field at all), which
     is one of the three things the initiation mode decides. #}
  {% if purpose %}
  <section class="purpose">
    <div class="caption">{{ purpose_label }}</div>
    <div data-field="payment_purpose">{{ purpose }}</div>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  {# THE CONDITIONAL BLOCK, and the whole of what "one template, not two archetypes" means.
     👁 A card operation prints an authorization code (4/4 of the card cases, always six digits)
     and a card number; a transfer by account details prints neither, because there was no card
     operation to authorize.

     ⛔ NO RRN — 👁 0 of 8. It is a requisite of the card operation as printed inside a FISCAL
     RECEIPT, where this repository does print it. ⛔ And no payment form (cash / card) — 👁 0 of
     8; that too is a receipt requisite, carried over by mistake in an earlier field list.

     👁 CARD MASKING IS NOT UNIFORM: six schemes over six documents, and one of them is no
     masking at all. The scheme is drawn in the builder. #}
  {% if auth_code or card_masked or terminal_value %}
  <section class="operation">
    {% if auth_code %}
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ auth_code_label }}</span>
      <span data-field="auth_code">{{ auth_code }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if card_masked %}
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ card_label }}</span>
      <span data-field="card_masked">{{ card_masked }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {# 👁 5/8 carry a device or initiating-system field, under five different captions whose
       values are of three incompatible kinds. The caption travels with the value, so one
       contract field covering three things is visible rather than hidden. #}
    {% if terminal_value %}
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ terminal_label }}</span>
      <span data-field="terminal">{{ terminal_value }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  {# 👁 THE STAMP IS THE ONE CONSTANT OF THIS CLASS — 8 of 8, and usually the bottom right. The
     signature beside it is 7/8; the exception printed an EMPTY SIGNATURE LINE, so the caption
     and the rule stay while the ink goes. The signature is geometry and spells no name: what
     the layout carries is that a document was signed, and a legible name in script would put a
     personal name on a published image for nothing.

     The stamp is not a `data-field` value — no label field claims it — but it does carry a box,
     like the receipt's discount line, so a consumer can find it without reading text. #}
  <section class="attestation">
    <div class="sign">
      <div class="sign-line">
        {% if signature_path %}
        <svg class="ink" viewBox="0 0 110 34" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="{{ signature_path }}" fill="none" stroke="#1b3a6b" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        </svg>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      <div class="muted">{{ signature_label }}</div>
      {% if signatory_post %}
      <div class="muted" data-field="signatory_post">{{ signatory_post }}</div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    <div class="stamp" data-field="stamp">
      <div class="stamp-inner">
        <div class="stamp-text">{{ stamp_caption }}</div>
        <div class="stamp-code">{{ bank_code }}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# TWO DIFFERENT MECHANISMS AT THE FOOT, and confusing them is the mistake worth avoiding.
     A note about the qualified electronic signature answers "is the signature under this file
     intact"; the verification footer answers "did this payment happen". 👁 3/8 carry the first
     (2/8 of one bank print a note about the operation being automatic INSTEAD), and 👁 3/8 carry
     the second — so neither is a constant, which refutes "every such document tells you how to
     check it".

     ⚠️ The verification host is the STATE cross-bank service rather than any bank's own page.
     👁 The observed footers name each bank's own address, and printing one here would be this
     repository asserting that pairing; the narrowing is declared in config/fiscal-rules.yaml.

     THE WHOLE FOOT IS CONDITIONAL, not merely its rows. Every part of it is a minority — 3/8,
     3/8 and 2/8 — so a document carrying none of them is ordinary, and an unconditional
     `<footer>` printed its top rule with nothing under it: a line across the page announcing an
     empty block. Correct and ugly is still a defect on every image that hits it. #}
  {% if electronic_note or verification_footer or qr_svg %}
  <footer class="foot">
    {% if electronic_note %}
    <div class="note" data-field="electronic_note">{{ electronic_note }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if verification_footer %}
    <div class="note">
      <span class="caption">{{ verification_caption }}: </span>
      <span data-field="verification_footer">{{ verification_footer }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {# 👁 2/8 carry a QR and BOTH are marketing — an invitation to install an application. 👁 0/8
       carry a verification QR: a document is checked by its code as text. ✅ So this class
       refutes "a QR means the document is fiscal" more sharply than the receipt does, where the
       QR at least addresses the tax service. #}
    {% if qr_svg %}
    <div class="promo">
      <div class="qr" data-field="qr">{{ qr_svg | safe }}</div>
      <div class="promo-text">{{ qr_caption }}</div>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </footer>
  {% endif %}

</div>
